<div class="wrap">

<header class="mast">
  <div class="eyebrow"><span>Feasibility research</span><span>2026-08-30</span><span>cache-ttl-analyzer</span></div>
  <h1>Can Claude Code logs price a different cache TTL?</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Before building a browser tool that replays session logs against 5-minute and 1-hour prompt-cache settings, we checked whether the logs actually carry what the arithmetic needs.</p>
</header>

<div class="verdict">
  <div class="stamp">Verdict</div>
  <div>
    <p><strong>Yes — the data is there. Build it.</strong> Every input the cost model needs is recorded per request. The <em>actual</em> cost of a session is fully determined by the logs; the <em>counterfactual</em> is a simulation with one honest approximation, and that approximation errs in the safe direction.</p>
    <p>This is confirmed by execution, not by reading a schema. A prototype simulator ran over seven real sessions end to end and returned differentiated recommendations — six of the seven were running on the wrong setting.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<section>
  <h2><span class="num">01</span> &nbsp;What the logs are</h2>
  <p class="h2sub">One JSONL file per session, one record per line</p>
  <p>Logs live at <code>~/.claude/projects/&lt;slugified-cwd&gt;/&lt;session-uuid&gt;.jsonl</code>. Observed record types include <code>assistant</code>, <code>user</code>, <code>attachment</code>, <code>system</code>, <code>file-history-snapshot</code>, <code>mode</code>, <code>ai-title</code> and several session-state markers.</p>
  <p>Only <code>type: "assistant"</code> rows carry billing data. Everything else the parser can ignore.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2><span class="num">02</span> &nbsp;The billing payload</h2>
  <p class="h2sub">Every assistant row carries a full <span class="mono">usage</span> block</p>
  <div class="scroll"><pre>{
  <span class="k">"input_tokens"</span>: 2,
  <span class="k">"cache_creation_input_tokens"</span>: 13185,
  <span class="k">"cache_read_input_tokens"</span>: 23472,
  <span class="k">"output_tokens"</span>: 386,
  <span class="k">"output_tokens_details"</span>: { <span class="k">"thinking_tokens"</span>: 237 },
  <span class="k">"cache_creation"</span>: {
    <span class="hi">"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0,</span>
    <span class="hi">"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 13185</span>
  },
  <span class="k">"service_tier"</span>: <span class="k">"standard"</span>,
  <span class="k">"speed"</span>: <span class="k">"standard"</span>
}</pre></div>
  <p>Alongside it on the row: <code>timestamp</code> (ISO-8601, millisecond), <code>requestId</code>, <code>message.id</code>, <code>message.model</code>, <code>isSidechain</code>, <code>sessionId</code>, <code>cwd</code>, <code>gitBranch</code>.</p>

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>What the model needs</th><th>Field</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Which model priced it</td><td class="mono">message.model</td><td>sessions mix models — price per request</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Uncached input</td><td class="mono">usage.input_tokens</td><td>full rate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cache reads</td><td class="mono">usage.cache_read_input_tokens</td><td>billed 0.1×</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cache writes</td><td class="mono">usage.cache_creation_input_tokens</td><td>1.25× at 5m, 2.0× at 1h</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Which TTL actually ran</strong></td><td class="mono">usage.cache_creation.ephemeral_5m / _1h</td><td>the decisive field</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Output</td><td class="mono">usage.output_tokens</td><td>thinking tokens already included</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Request timing</td><td class="mono">timestamp</td><td>preceding <code>user</code> row approximates request <em>start</em></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rate modifiers</td><td class="mono">usage.service_tier, usage.speed</td><td><code>speed:"fast"</code> on Opus 5 prices at $10/$50</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <p><strong>The configured TTL is never written down.</strong> It is not in the log and not in <code>settings.json</code> — it has to be inferred from the <code>cache_creation</code> split. That inference held up: across all seven sessions, 100% of cache-write tokens landed in <code>ephemeral_1h_input_tokens</code>, correctly identifying a 1-hour configuration. Treat it as a token-weighted mix rather than a boolean, since server tools can insert their own 5-minute writes into a request.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2><span class="num">03</span> &nbsp;The arithmetic</h2>
  <p class="h2sub">Two multipliers and a refresh rule decide everything</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Cache <strong>read</strong> = 0.1× the base input rate.</li>
    <li>Cache <strong>write</strong> = <span class="pill p5">1.25× at 5m</span> &nbsp;<span class="pill p1">2.0× at 1h</span></li>
    <li>A cache read <strong>refreshes the entry's timer at no cost</strong>, on either TTL.</li>
    <li>Lifetime runs from the <strong>start</strong> of the request that writes or reads it — generation time counts against the window.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Break-even follows directly: 5m pays for itself at two requests (1.25 + 0.1 vs 2.0 uncached); 1h needs three or more (2.0 + 0.2 vs 3.0). So <strong>1h only wins when gaps exceed five minutes</strong> — below that, every request refreshes the 5-minute entry for free and the doubled write price is pure loss. Quantifying exactly that tradeoff is the product.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2><span class="num">04</span> &nbsp;Traps</h2>
  <p class="h2sub">Four ways a naive parser produces confidently wrong dollars</p>

  <div class="traps">
    <div class="trap">
      <h3>Rows are content blocks, not messages <span class="pill pd">up to 13× overcount</span></h3>
      <p>Claude Code writes <strong>one row per content block</strong> of an assistant message, and every one of those rows repeats the <em>full message</em> <code>usage</code>. In the sample, one message id appears <strong>13 times</strong>, each row claiming the same 12,528 output tokens and 37,044 cache reads. Summing rows inflates the bill by the block count. Across the sample, 281 rows collapse to 142 real requests.</p>
      <p>Deduplicate on <code>message.id</code>. It maps 1:1 with <code>requestId</code> across all 669 assistant rows checked, so either key works.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="trap">
      <h3>Synthetic rows are not billable</h3>
      <p>Rows with <code>message.model == "&lt;synthetic&gt;"</code> come from API error paths, carry no <code>service_tier</code> or <code>speed</code>, and represent no call. Exclude them.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="trap">
      <h3>Deduplication has to be global across files</h3>
      <p><code>--resume</code> and forking can copy history into a new session file. Not observed in this sample — zero message ids appeared in more than one file — but a folder upload must dedupe across the whole set, not per file.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="trap">
      <h3>Sidechains are a separate cache namespace <span class="pill pd">untested path</span></h3>
      <p><code>isSidechain: true</code> marks subagent turns. A subagent runs its own conversation with its own prefix, so its cache lifetime is independent of the main thread and gaps must be computed within each thread separately. No sidechains appear in the available logs, so this branch will stay untested until a session with subagents is deliberately captured as a fixture.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2><span class="num">05</span> &nbsp;The one real approximation</h2>
  <p class="h2sub">Aggregate token counts can't resolve partial expiry</p>
  <div class="caveat">
    <p>Claude Code uses up to four cache breakpoints, but the log reports a single <code>cache_read_input_tokens</code> total. When a 5-minute entry lapses, real behavior is a <em>partial</em> invalidation — some breakpoints survive, some don't — and the logs cannot distinguish that. The simulator therefore models expiry as all-or-nothing: if the gap since the previous same-thread request exceeds the TTL, the tokens that were read become a fresh write at the write multiplier.</p>
    <p>This <strong>overstates</strong> the penalty of the shorter TTL, so a 5m recommendation is conservative — the tool will under-sell 5m rather than over-sell it. That is the right direction to be wrong in, and it belongs in the UI rather than hidden in a footnote.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Two smaller effects. The assistant <code>timestamp</code> is response completion, but TTL runs from request start; the preceding <code>user</code> row sits 2–5 seconds earlier and is a good proxy — cheap to use, so use it. And the simulation is only sensitive in the 5m-to-1h gap band at all: in this sample exactly one session had gaps in that band, and it is the only session where 1h won.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2><span class="num">06</span> &nbsp;Prototype results</h2>
  <p class="h2sub">Seven real sessions, priced both ways</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Session</th><th class="n">Requests</th><th class="n">Span</th><th class="n">Cost @ 5m</th><th class="n">Cost @ 1h</th><th>Better</th><th class="n">Delta</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="mono">908ed721</td><td class="n">8</td><td class="n">1.1 m</td><td class="n">$0.77</td><td class="n">$1.07</td><td><span class="pill p5">5m</span></td><td class="n">$0.30</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">3dd62ac3</td><td class="n">19</td><td class="n">10.7 m</td><td class="n">$1.93</td><td class="n">$2.32</td><td><span class="pill p5">5m</span></td><td class="n">$0.40</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">43e08bdd</td><td class="n">50</td><td class="n">80.2 m</td><td class="n">$6.97</td><td class="n">$4.83</td><td><span class="pill p1">1h</span></td><td class="n">$2.13</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">334db6f6</td><td class="n">142</td><td class="n">29.1 m</td><td class="n">$11.86</td><td class="n">$12.80</td><td><span class="pill p5">5m</span></td><td class="n">$0.94</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">4cb8948e</td><td class="n">56</td><td class="n">19.2 m</td><td class="n">$4.63</td><td class="n">$5.10</td><td><span class="pill p5">5m</span></td><td class="n">$0.47</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">d4592e2c</td><td class="n">10</td><td class="n">2.9 m</td><td class="n">$0.83</td><td class="n">$1.07</td><td><span class="pill p5">5m</span></td><td class="n">$0.24</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">98d37497</td><td class="n">11</td><td class="n">1.3 m</td><td class="n">$0.54</td><td class="n">$0.71</td><td><span class="pill p5">5m</span></td><td class="n">$0.16</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>The result is not degenerate — it flips on session shape. Tight agent loops favor 5m, because writes are half price and every read refreshes the window. The 80-minute session, full of human think-time gaps, favors 1h by 31%. All seven of these sessions actually ran at 1h, so six were on the wrong setting.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2><span class="num">07</span> &nbsp;Privacy</h2>
  <p class="h2sub">Local-only processing is required, not decorative</p>
  <p>The JSONL contains full user prompts, full assistant output, complete file contents via <code>attachment</code> and <code>file-history-snapshot</code> records, absolute paths, git branch names, and tool output. Uploading one would be a real disclosure, so the client-side-only design is the correct call and worth stating prominently.</p>
  <p>Usefully, the parser never needs to read <code>message.content</code> at all — only <code>usage</code> and a handful of metadata fields. That is worth enforcing in code as a defensive property rather than leaving as a convention.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2><span class="num">08</span> &nbsp;Open before build</h2>
  <p class="h2sub">Four things to settle, none of them blocking</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>JSON input, as distinct from JSONL.</strong> Claude Code's <code>/export</code> output is a different shape and may not carry <code>usage</code> at all. Verify against a real export before promising support; JSONL is confirmed and should be the primary path.</li>
    <li><strong>Capture two fixtures</strong> the local logs can't provide: a session with subagents, to exercise the sidechain path, and a session actually configured at 5m, to test the inverse inference.</li>
    <li><strong>Decide on a hybrid recommendation</strong> — 1h on the stable system and tool prefix, 5m on the conversation tail. The logs cannot resolve per-breakpoint behavior, so this can only ever be presented as guidance, never as a computed number.</li>
    <li><strong>Pin the pricing table</strong> with a visible "rates as of" date and a one-file update path.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<footer>
  <span>Sources: 7 sessions · 669 assistant rows · 296 deduplicated requests</span>
  <span>docs/design/log-format-feasibility.md</span>
</footer>

</div>
